% ── Title ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
\title{\textbf{IRE Assignment 2: Learning from Click-Logs}\\[4pt]
       \large Two-Stage News Recommendation on EB-NeRD and MIND}
\author{
  % TODO: Replace with your name(s) and roll number(s)
  Name 1 (Roll No.) \and Name 2 (Roll No.)
}
\date{September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Introduction}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

We present a two-stage retrieve-then-rank news recommendation pipeline evaluated on two datasets: \textbf{EB-NeRD}~\cite{kruse2024ebnerd} (Ekstra Bladet News Recommendation Dataset) and \textbf{MIND}~\cite{wu2020mind} (MIcrosoft News Dataset).
Our system first generates candidates via a popularity-based retriever, then re-ranks them using a LightGBM gradient-boosted decision tree trained over hand-crafted behavioural features.
We reproduce a popularity baseline, introduce category-aware features as a principled improvement, validate the gain with paired bootstrap confidence intervals, and analyse serving characteristics at scale.


% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Q1: Click-History \& Session Features}
\label{sec:features}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

We engineer 11 features from the click-logs, grouped into four categories.
All features respect the behavioural-window boundary: only clicks that precede the current impression in timestamp order are used, preventing any future-information leakage.

\subsection{Feature Taxonomy}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Engineered feature set. Features marked with $\dagger$ are the ablated category-aware feature.}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Group} & \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Description} \\
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{Click-History} 
  & \texttt{click\_count} & Number of past clicks by the user \\
  & \texttt{recency\_weighted\_score} & Exponentially decayed history score ($t_{1/2}=24$h) \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{Article} 
  & \texttt{article\_popularity} & Raw click count of the candidate article \\
  & \texttt{log\_popularity} & $\log(1 + \text{click\_count})$ \\
  & \texttt{article\_freshness\_hours} & Hours since article publication \\
  & \texttt{freshness\_bucket} & Binned freshness (0--4 scale) \\
  & \texttt{category\_popularity} & Avg.\ popularity of articles in same category \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{Session} 
  & \texttt{session\_click\_count} & Clicks so far in the current session \\
  & \texttt{session\_position} & Ordinal position within the session \\
  & \texttt{position\_in\_inview} & Position of the candidate in the impression list \\
\midrule
Category$^\dagger$ & \texttt{category\_match} & Binary: does the candidate's category match user history? \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Behavioural-Window Boundary Enforcement}

To prevent data leakage, we enforce strict temporal boundaries:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{EB-NeRD}: Training timestamps end at \texttt{2023-05-25 06:59:52}; validation begins at \texttt{2023-05-25 07:00:15} — a clean temporal split with no overlap.
  \item \textbf{MIND}: Training ends at \texttt{2019-11-09 09:59:58}; validation begins at \texttt{2019-11-15 10:00:00}.
  \item User histories are built exclusively from the training split; popularity indices are computed from training clicks only.
  \item The \texttt{compute\_features\_for\_impression()} function receives only past-available data at both train and serve time.
  \item All features used (popularity, category, freshness, user history) are available at serving time — no oracle features.
\end{itemize}


% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Q2: Re-Ranker}
\label{sec:reranker}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

\subsection{Architecture: Retrieve-then-Rank}

Our pipeline follows a two-stage design:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Stage 1 — Candidate Generation}: A popularity-based retriever selects the top-$K$ candidates ($K \approx 100\text{--}200$) from the article pool. This is an $O(1)$ lookup per candidate using a pre-computed popularity index.
  \item \textbf{Stage 2 — Re-Ranking}: A LightGBM~\cite{ke2017lightgbm} binary classifier scores each candidate using the 11 behavioural features from Section~\ref{sec:features}. Candidates are re-ordered by predicted click probability.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{LightGBM Configuration}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{LightGBM hyperparameters.}
\label{tab:lgbm}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Parameter} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Objective & Binary cross-entropy \\
Metric & AUC \\
Learning rate & 0.05 \\
Number of leaves & 63 \\
Max depth & 7 \\
Min child samples & 50 \\
Feature fraction & 0.8 \\
Bagging fraction / freq & 0.8 / 5 \\
Max rounds & 300 \\
Early stopping & 30 rounds \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Training Statistics}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Training set statistics for the full re-ranker model.}
\label{tab:training}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Train Samples} & \textbf{Val Samples} & \textbf{Pos.\ Rate} & \textbf{Best AUC} \\
\midrule
EB-NeRD & 278,139 & 300,845 & 8.9\% & 0.7024 \\
MIND    & 5,843,444 & 2,740,998 & 4.0\% & 0.5940 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Q3: Baseline Reproduced, Then Beaten}
\label{sec:ablation}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

\subsection{Baseline: Popularity Ranking}

Our baseline ranks candidates by their global click popularity — the simplest non-personalised strategy. This serves as the ``official'' starting point against which all improvements are measured.

\subsection{Improvement: Category-Aware Feature}

The principled improvement is the addition of a \textbf{category\_match} feature, a binary indicator that fires when a candidate article's category appears in the user's click history. This captures topical affinity without requiring embedding similarity, making it efficient and interpretable.

\subsection{Ablation Study}

We train two models:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Full model}: All 11 features including \texttt{category\_match}.
  \item \textbf{Ablated model}: 10 features, with \texttt{category\_match} removed.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{EB-NeRD Results}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{EB-NeRD: Overall metrics with 95\% bootstrap CIs (1,000 iterations).}
\label{tab:ebnerd-overall}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Popularity (Baseline)} & \textbf{Reranker (Ablated)} & \textbf{Reranker (Full)} \\
\midrule
AUC        & 0.4662\,[0.465, 0.468]  & 0.5851\,[0.581, 0.589]  & \textbf{0.6409}\,[0.637, 0.645] \\
MRR        & 0.2784\,[0.275, 0.282]  & 0.3537\,[0.350, 0.357]  & \textbf{0.4059}\,[0.402, 0.410] \\
nDCG@5     & 0.3075\,[0.304, 0.312]  & 0.4003\,[0.396, 0.404]  & \textbf{0.4578}\,[0.454, 0.462] \\
nDCG@10    & 0.3992\,[0.396, 0.403]  & 0.4768\,[0.474, 0.480]  & \textbf{0.5190}\,[0.515, 0.523] \\
Novelty    & 1.627\,[1.599, 1.656]   & 1.105\,[1.082, 1.128]   & 1.075\,[1.053, 1.099] \\
Coverage   & 0.220                   & 0.206                   & 0.206 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{MIND Results}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{MIND: Overall metrics with 95\% bootstrap CIs (1,000 iterations).}
\label{tab:mind-overall}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Popularity (Baseline)} & \textbf{Reranker (Ablated)} & \textbf{Reranker (Full)} \\
\midrule
AUC        & 0.5318\,[0.530, 0.534]  & 0.5094\,[0.508, 0.511]  & \textbf{0.5382}\,[0.536, 0.540] \\
MRR        & 0.2666\,[0.265, 0.269]  & 0.2587\,[0.257, 0.261]  & \textbf{0.2743}\,[0.272, 0.276] \\
nDCG@5     & 0.2454\,[0.243, 0.248]  & 0.2370\,[0.234, 0.239]  & \textbf{0.2568}\,[0.254, 0.259] \\
nDCG@10    & 0.3092\,[0.307, 0.312]  & 0.2991\,[0.297, 0.301]  & \textbf{0.3159}\,[0.314, 0.318] \\
Novelty    & 8.842\,[8.812, 8.874]   & 6.450\,[6.423, 6.476]   & 9.162\,[9.131, 9.196] \\
Coverage   & 0.031                   & 0.048                   & 0.034 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Statistical Significance}

All improvements are validated using paired bootstrap tests (1,000 iterations, 95\% CI). A gain is deemed significant if the confidence interval for the difference excludes zero.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Paired bootstrap $\Delta$ (Full $-$ Ablated) with 95\% CIs. All intervals exclude zero.}
\label{tab:significance}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{EB-NeRD}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{MIND}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
\textbf{Metric} & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
AUC     & +0.0558 & [0.052, 0.059] & +0.0287 & [0.027, 0.030] \\
MRR     & +0.0522 & [0.048, 0.056] & +0.0155 & [0.014, 0.017] \\
nDCG@5  & +0.0574 & [0.054, 0.061] & +0.0198 & [0.018, 0.022] \\
nDCG@10 & +0.0422 & [0.039, 0.045] & +0.0168 & [0.015, 0.018] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Paired bootstrap $\Delta$ (Full $-$ Baseline) with 95\% CIs. All intervals exclude zero.}
\label{tab:significance-baseline}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{EB-NeRD}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{MIND}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
\textbf{Metric} & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI & $\Delta$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
AUC     & +0.1746 & [0.171, 0.179] & +0.0064 & [0.005, 0.008] \\
MRR     & +0.1275 & [0.123, 0.132] & +0.0076 & [0.006, 0.009] \\
nDCG@5  & +0.1502 & [0.145, 0.156] & +0.0114 & [0.010, 0.013] \\
nDCG@10 & +0.1198 & [0.116, 0.124] & +0.0067 & [0.006, 0.008] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key observation}: The category-match feature contributes a substantial lift on EB-NeRD ($+5.6$ AUC points) and a moderate but statistically significant lift on MIND ($+2.9$ AUC points). All confidence intervals exclude zero, confirming the gains are not due to chance.


% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Q4: Serving \& Scale Analysis}
\label{sec:serving}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

\subsection{Index Memory}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Feature store memory footprint (in-memory, single process).}
\label{tab:memory}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Component} & \textbf{EB-NeRD (MB)} & \textbf{MIND (MB)} \\
\midrule
Popularity index     & 0.07  & 0.58 \\
User histories       & 9.51  & 57.74 \\
Articles DataFrame   & 48.73 & 50.17 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Total}       & \textbf{58.31} & \textbf{108.49} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Latency}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{End-to-end per-request latency (candidate generation + re-ranking).}
\label{tab:latency}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Percentile} & \textbf{EB-NeRD (ms)} & \textbf{MIND (ms)} \\
\midrule
p50  & 0.085 & 0.066 \\
p95  & 2.432 & 0.119 \\
p99  & 6.179 & 0.243 \\
Mean & 0.457 & 0.088 \\
Max  & 15.18 & 11.78 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Both systems comfortably meet a \textbf{p99 $<$ 100\,ms SLA}. The EB-NeRD p99 is higher due to larger candidate lists per impression.

\subsection{Cost / QPS Estimate}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Back-of-envelope cost at target SLA (p99 $<$ 100\,ms).}
\label{tab:cost}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
 & \textbf{EB-NeRD} & \textbf{MIND} \\
\midrule
QPS per core        & 2,188   & 11,351 \\
Cost per 1K queries & \$6$\times 10^{-6}$ & \$1$\times 10^{-6}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At current scale, a single CPU core can serve thousands of queries per second. Even at peak load, the cost is negligible — well under \$0.01 per million queries.

\subsection{Scaling to 10$\times$}

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Memory}: At 10$\times$, the EB-NeRD feature store grows to $\sim$583\,MB and MIND to $\sim$1,085\,MB — still fits in RAM on a single 64\,GB machine.
  \item \textbf{Compute}: The re-ranker costs $\sim$0.1--0.5\,ms per request; 10$\times$ QPS requires $\sim$10 cores — still a single multi-core server.
  \item \textbf{First bottleneck}: \textbf{Feature store memory}. At 100$\times$, user histories alone would exceed single-machine RAM.
  \item \textbf{Mitigations}: (1) LRU eviction of inactive users, (2) external key-value store (Redis/Memcached), (3) horizontal sharding by user\_id hash across 2--4 nodes.
  \item \textbf{Candidate generation}: Popularity lookup is $O(1)$ per candidate — no bottleneck even at 100$\times$.
\end{itemize}


% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Q5: Extended Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

We report all metrics with two slicing dimensions: \textbf{cold-start vs.\ warm} users ($\leq 5$ clicks vs.\ $>5$ clicks) and \textbf{head vs.\ tail} articles (top 20\% by popularity vs.\ remaining).

\subsection{EB-NeRD Sliced Results}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{EB-NeRD: Full re-ranker metrics by user/article slice.}
\label{tab:ebnerd-sliced}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Cold-Start} ($n$=3,951) & \textbf{Warm} ($n$=21,049) & \textbf{Head} ($n$=35) & \textbf{Tail} ($n$=24,965) \\
\midrule
AUC     & 0.604\,[0.595, 0.612] & 0.648\,[0.644, 0.652] & 0.642\,[0.545, 0.735] & 0.641\,[0.637, 0.644] \\
MRR     & 0.372\,[0.363, 0.381] & 0.412\,[0.408, 0.417] & 0.484\,[0.391, 0.579] & 0.406\,[0.402, 0.410] \\
nDCG@5  & 0.424\,[0.414, 0.434] & 0.464\,[0.459, 0.469] & 0.601\,[0.521, 0.681] & 0.458\,[0.453, 0.462] \\
nDCG@10 & 0.494\,[0.485, 0.501] & 0.524\,[0.520, 0.528] & 0.611\,[0.540, 0.684] & 0.519\,[0.515, 0.522] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Observations}: Warm users benefit more from the re-ranker ($+4.4$ AUC points over cold-start), as expected — more history yields richer features. Head articles show higher nDCG because popularity features are more informative for frequently clicked items.

