\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Cold-Start} ($n$=65,495) & \textbf{Warm} ($n$=7,657) & \textbf{Head} ($n$=1,448) & \textbf{Tail} ($n$=71,704) \\
\midrule
AUC     & 0.539\,[0.537, 0.541] & 0.529\,[0.522, 0.536] & 0.550\,[0.536, 0.563] & 0.538\,[0.536, 0.540] \\
MRR     & 0.273\,[0.270, 0.275] & 0.286\,[0.279, 0.293] & 0.274\,[0.259, 0.288] & 0.274\,[0.272, 0.277] \\
nDCG@5  & 0.256\,[0.253, 0.259] & 0.263\,[0.256, 0.271] & 0.257\,[0.239, 0.274] & 0.257\,[0.254, 0.259] \\
nDCG@10 & 0.315\,[0.313, 0.318] & 0.322\,[0.314, 0.329] & 0.330\,[0.314, 0.345] & 0.316\,[0.313, 0.318] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Observations}: MIND's cold-start population is much larger (89.5\% of users), diluting the warm-user advantage. The re-ranker still provides a consistent lift over the popularity baseline across all slices, with warm users and head articles showing the strongest gains.

\subsection{Diversity and Beyond-Accuracy Metrics}

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Novelty and coverage comparison across methods and datasets.}
\label{tab:diversity}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{EB-NeRD}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{MIND}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Novelty} & \textbf{Coverage} & \textbf{Novelty} & \textbf{Coverage} \\
\midrule
Popularity  & 1.63 & 0.220 & 8.84 & 0.031 \\
Reranker    & 1.08 & 0.206 & 9.16 & 0.034 \\
