\section{Q6: Design Note}
\label{sec:design}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

\subsection{What We Built}

A fully reproducible, end-to-end news recommendation pipeline consisting of:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Data loader} (\texttt{data\_loader.py}): Downloads, extracts, and parses both EB-NeRD (Parquet) and MIND (TSV) datasets with automatic handling of nested directory structures and ragged CSV lines.
  \item \textbf{Feature engineering} (\texttt{feature\_engineering.py}): Computes 11 behavioural features with strict temporal boundary enforcement.
  \item \textbf{Candidate generation} (\texttt{candidate\_generation.py}): Popularity-based top-$K$ retrieval.
  \item \textbf{Re-ranker} (\texttt{reranker.py}): LightGBM binary classifier with early stopping.
  \item \textbf{Evaluation harness} (\texttt{evaluation.py}): AUC, MRR, nDCG@$k$, novelty, coverage with bootstrap CIs and sliced analysis.
  \item \textbf{Serving analysis} (\texttt{serving\_analysis.py}): Memory profiling, latency benchmarking, cost estimation.
  \item \textbf{Prediction generator} (\texttt{predict.py}): Batch prediction for Codabench submission with progress tracking.
  \item \textbf{Pipeline orchestrator} (\texttt{run\_pipeline.py}): One-command end-to-end execution.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Key Design Choices}

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{LightGBM over neural rankers}: We chose Option A (GBDT) for interpretability, fast training ($<$1 minute), and competitive performance without GPU requirements. The feature importance is directly inspectable, aiding the ablation study.
  \item \textbf{Popularity-based retrieval}: While embedding-based ANN retrieval would improve recall, the popularity-based approach ensures reproducibility and isolates the re-ranker's contribution.
  \item \textbf{Category-match as the improvement}: This feature was chosen because (a) it captures a strong signal (topical affinity), (b) it is cheap to compute at serving time ($O(1)$ set lookup), and (c) it is available for both datasets without requiring pre-trained embeddings.
  \item \textbf{Polars + PyArrow}: We use Polars for data manipulation and PyArrow for batch Parquet reading, enabling memory-efficient processing of the 13.5M-impression EB-NeRD test set.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Alternatives Considered}

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{NRMS / neural user encoder}: Would likely yield stronger results on MIND (per~\cite{wu2019nrms}), but requires GPU and pre-trained word embeddings, complicating reproducibility.
  \item \textbf{XGBoost}: Comparable to LightGBM but slower on categorical features; we found no meaningful difference in pilot experiments.
  \item \textbf{Embedding similarity features}: Using pre-computed BERT/Word2Vec embeddings for user--article similarity. Deferred to avoid dependency on large artifact downloads.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Where It Breaks at 10$\times$}

At 10$\times$ the current user base:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{Feature store memory} is the first bottleneck ($\sim$583\,MB for EB-NeRD, $\sim$1,085\,MB for MIND). Mitigation: LRU eviction or external KV store.
  \item \textbf{Compute} remains manageable: 10 cores handle 10$\times$ QPS on a single machine.
  \item At \textbf{100$\times$}: horizontal sharding by user\_id across 2--4 nodes becomes necessary, with each shard maintaining a local feature store and shared model replicas.
  \item The \textbf{popularity index} and \textbf{LightGBM model} are both lightweight ($<$1\,MB) and trivially replicated.
\end{itemize}


% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\section{Anti-Gaming Statement}
% ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item No future-click leakage: all features are computed from temporally prior data only.
  \item All features used are available at serving time (no oracle features such as ground-truth labels or future click counts).
  \item The test sets (EB-NeRD and MIND) do not contain click labels; predictions are generated using the trained model without access to ground truth.
  \item Metrics are reported with and without the category-match feature (ablation) to isolate its contribution.
\end{itemize}


